  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: a6334982dcae08e4698af5d208c769f4, type: 3}
  m_Name: Question2_Valley
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  description:
  - "Painted in the autumn of 1889 while van Gogh was at Saint R\xE9my, this work
    uses a less common viewpoint. The viewer looks down into a valley, over fields,
    tracks, a house, and a lone ploughman. The elevated angle brings out pattern
    and rhythm in the land, linking human labor with the larger sweep of nature.
    The scene reflects van Gogh\u2019s interest in agricultural life and in how humans
    work within natural rhythms. The painting is not a strict literal view. His compositional
    choices underline his expressive aims."
  - In closing, Valley with Ploughman Seen from Above shows how van Gogh could take
    a quiet rural subject and fill it with movement and rhythm. His choice of viewpoint,
    expressive brushwork, strong yet controlled colors, and the inclusion of a working
    figure all work together to make the painting more than a countryside scene.
    It becomes a reflection on human engagement with nature, on patterns in the land,
    and on the sense of motion that exists even within stillness.
  awnsers:
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: 5261ba4d5ca2d30488f3d20543e8a2f4, type: 3}
  - {fileID: 8300000, guid: e25dc54cd38d91e4585cf8d7cf9d303e, type: 3}
